WEBVTT

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:02.840
Ever find yourself switching
between a terminal,

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:05.660
a file browser, and a page of notes?

00:00:06.254 --> 00:00:08.524
That's where WebSSH comes in.

00:00:08.714 --> 00:00:13.604
It brings those everyday tools into one
workspace, running on your own server.

00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:16.775
You install it on a machine you control.

00:00:17.185 --> 00:00:21.535
Your browser talks to that instance, and
that instance connects to your servers.

00:00:22.303 --> 00:00:23.953
Let's start with Overview.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:27.061
Give a saved connection a useful name,

00:00:27.211 --> 00:00:30.181
and you don't have to remember
its address every time.

00:00:30.707 --> 00:00:31.837
Looking for production?

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:34.637
Type it into search, or pick a group.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:37.097
Recent connections also make it easy

00:00:37.107 --> 00:00:39.777
to get back to a machine
you were just using.

00:00:40.269 --> 00:00:44.839
Switch to the route view, and you can see
how your saved connections fit together.

00:00:45.249 --> 00:00:48.619
Here, a few destinations
go through a jump host.

00:00:49.337 --> 00:00:52.867
Need a connection you haven't
saved yet? Open Quick Connect.

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:57.860
Enter the host, port, and remote
username. Nothing surprising here.

00:00:58.282 --> 00:01:00.852
You can use a password or a saved key.

00:01:01.182 --> 00:01:03.732
We'll pick the operations
key for this example.

00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:07.135
If the destination sits
behind a jump host,

00:01:07.425 --> 00:01:09.355
choose that in the advanced settings.

00:01:09.745 --> 00:01:12.375
The extra options are
there when you need them.

00:01:12.803 --> 00:01:16.833
Before you connect, compare the host's
fingerprint with a trusted source.

00:01:17.303 --> 00:01:19.473
Check that you're reaching
the right machine.

00:01:20.203 --> 00:01:23.713
Now for the part you'll probably
spend most of your time in:

00:01:23.763 --> 00:01:25.213
the terminal workspace.

00:01:25.837 --> 00:01:30.247
Each connection gets a tab.
Let's put two sessions side by side,

00:01:30.257 --> 00:01:34.007
with the edge server on the left
and the build runner on the right.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.160
Looking for a particular line?

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.460
Open terminal search and
type what you're looking for.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:42.000
Here, we're finding the web container.

00:01:42.437 --> 00:01:45.767
And if you need more room,
switch to four panes.

00:01:46.037 --> 00:01:48.407
Now all four sessions
are visible together.

00:01:49.137 --> 00:01:52.947
Let's open Files. You get
secure remote file access,

00:01:52.957 --> 00:01:55.527
right alongside the terminal workflow.

00:01:56.107 --> 00:01:59.057
The two panes have
separate sources and paths.

00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:02.877
That makes it easy to keep an eye
on where something is going.

00:02:03.401 --> 00:02:06.171
Pick a text file, like
these deployment notes.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.980
You can preview it here,
then switch into edit mode.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.100
No need to download it
just to change a line.

00:02:13.561 --> 00:02:15.331
We'll add one item to the checklist.

00:02:15.341 --> 00:02:20.191
Notice the unsaved marker:
the edit hasn't been written back yet.

00:02:20.622 --> 00:02:22.052
Back in the file workspace,

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:25.352
the transfer queue keeps
progress and status visible

00:02:25.362 --> 00:02:27.392
when you're moving several files.

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:30.973
We all have commands we run over and over.

00:02:31.303 --> 00:02:33.593
This is where the command library helps.

00:02:34.409 --> 00:02:36.379
Command sets take that one step further:

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:40.299
several commands, arranged
into a reusable sequence.

00:02:40.799 --> 00:02:44.489
This baseline check already
includes uptime and service status.

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:46.689
Let's open it and have a look.

00:02:47.269 --> 00:02:50.639
We'll find the disk-usage command
and add it as the third step.

00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:52.759
There's the updated order.

00:02:53.425 --> 00:02:56.995
Before running a set, check
the commands and the target session.

00:02:58.055 --> 00:03:00.565
Use the notes as a private scratchpad.

00:03:00.815 --> 00:03:02.905
A release checklist, a reminder,

00:03:03.215 --> 00:03:05.485
whatever helps you pick
up where you left off.

00:03:05.755 --> 00:03:07.725
They're shared across your own sessions.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:11.240
Open diagnostics to check resource usage

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.380
and inspect services on
a compatible Linux host.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.520
These are sample measurements.

00:03:17.078 --> 00:03:20.088
The details you can see
depend on the remote system

00:03:20.098 --> 00:03:22.028
and your account's permissions.

00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:26.026
Depending on your setup,
you can enable passkeys,

00:03:26.156 --> 00:03:29.906
an authenticator app,
or an external identity provider.

00:03:30.469 --> 00:03:32.939
Set up recovery while
you can still sign in.

00:03:32.949 --> 00:03:36.679
It's much nicer to have that
ready before you need it.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.800
Ready to have a look yourself?

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:43.280
Head to getwebssh dot com and
open the Docker quickstart.

00:03:43.861 --> 00:03:48.711
It uses the published container image
and a persistent volume for your data.

00:03:49.377 --> 00:03:52.547
The example keeps the web
port on your own computer.

00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:56.947
That's a useful way to explore without
opening it to the whole network.

00:03:57.574 --> 00:04:01.424
Start the container, open
localhost on port five thousand,

00:04:01.894 --> 00:04:05.124
and create the first
administrator on a fresh instance.

00:04:05.697 --> 00:04:08.587
For internet access,
follow the production guide.

00:04:08.977 --> 00:04:12.587
It covers encrypted web
access, allowed origins,

00:04:12.757 --> 00:04:15.647
secure cookies, and the proxy
and network settings.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:21.457
Found something useful, or something you'd
like to improve? Come share it on GitHub.

00:04:22.329 --> 00:04:24.099
And if you enjoyed the walkthrough,

00:04:24.249 --> 00:04:27.599
subscribe for more practical
tools for your own infrastructure.

00:04:28.229 --> 00:04:29.439
Thanks for watching.
